<BaseLayout
  title="Ideas"
  description="Research topics and internship ideas."
>
  <h1>Ideas</h1>

  <p class="muted">
    If you’re interested in one of these topics, please <a href="/contact">get in touch</a>. This list is a subset of what I plan to work on someday, so it might as well be all together.
  </p>

  <p class="muted">
    Each idea includes two (totally arbitrary) scores out of 100:
    <strong>Difficulty</strong> (how challenging technically) and <strong>Importance</strong> (how much impact it
    could have for theory and/or practical tooling). These are just guidance.
  </p>

  <h2>Qudit circuits</h2>

  <p class="muted">
    A <strong>qudit</strong> is a <em>d</em>-level quantum system (a qubit is <em>d=2</em>, a qutrit is <em>d=3</em>).
    <strong>Clifford</strong> circuits are the structured gate family behind stabilizer error correction and many
    compilation and verification methods.
  </p>

  <h3>Clifford circuit axiomatization</h3>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>Axiomatization of single-qupit Clifford circuits (one wire)</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">55/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">70/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Build a <em>rewrite-friendly</em> “dictionary” of identities for the
        <strong>1‑qupit Clifford group</strong> (prime dimension <em>p</em>).
        Concretely, the project should deliver:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          a small set of generators (e.g., Fourier/phase-like gates) and a finite set of relations (rewrite rules);
        </li>
        <li>
          a <strong>canonical normal form</strong> (every 1‑qupit Clifford reduces to a unique, standard circuit);
        </li>
        <li>
          an implementation that takes a 1‑wire Clifford circuit and <strong>normalizes</strong> it automatically.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters because 1‑wire is the cleanest “sandbox” where you can make proofs rigorous and build tooling
        that later scales to multi‑qudit compilation and verification.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> the structure of qudit Clifford operations is well understood at the
        group-theory level in arbitrary dimensions, and there are constructive characterizations and generating sets.
        <strong>Still open (tooling-wise):</strong> turning that understanding into a <em>small</em>, implementation-ready
        rewrite system with a clearly stated normal form, plus automated tests and (ideally) machine-checked proofs.
        (<a href={refs.quditCliffordArbitraryD} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">quant-ph/0506065</a>,
        <a href={refs.quditCliffordIdeal} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1307.5087</a>,
        <a href={refs.qubitCliffordPresentation} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1310.6813</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>Axiomatization of Clifford circuits for qupits (prime dimension p)</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">75/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">85/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Design a compact, <em>sound and complete</em> set of circuit identities
        (generators + relations / rewrite rules) for <strong>multi-qupit</strong> Clifford circuits (prime
        dimension <em>p</em>), in a form suitable for <strong>automated rewriting</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        “Sound and complete” here means:
        (1) every rule is correct as a matrix identity, and
        (2) every true equality of qupit Clifford circuits can be derived using the rules.
        This is important because prime‑dimension qudits are the “clean” algebraic setting and a natural stepping stone
        toward composite dimensions used in real devices (e.g., ququarts).
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> there is now a complete rewrite rule set for <em>multi‑qutrit</em> Clifford
        circuits (a landmark result for odd prime dimension), and the general Clifford group structure for arbitrary
        dimensions is documented in several foundational works.
        <strong>Still open:</strong> a dimension‑parametric presentation that works for general prime <em>p</em> (not only
        <em>p=3</em>), with a normal form and an implementation that scales.
        (<a href={refs.qutritCliffordRules} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2508.14670</a>,
        <a href={refs.quditCliffordArbitraryD} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">quant-ph/0506065</a>,
        <a href={refs.quditCliffordIdeal} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1307.5087</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>Axiomatization of Clifford circuits for qudits (arbitrary dimension d, including composite)</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">90/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">90/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Extend Clifford-circuit axiomatizations beyond prime <em>p</em> to
        <strong>arbitrary dimension d</strong> (including composite <em>d</em>), and produce:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>a clear choice of generators for “arbitrary‑d Clifford circuits” (including the right phase conventions),</li>
        <li>a practical <strong>normal form</strong> (what does a “canonical Clifford circuit” look like in composite d?),</li>
        <li>
          an implementation strategy (e.g., reduction by rewriting + a decision procedure),
          and a benchmark suite.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters because many realistic platforms and encodings naturally produce composite dimensions (e.g. <em>d=4</em>),
        and dimension‑generic compilation/verification is a key missing piece for qudit tooling.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> the qudit stabilizer formalism and Clifford operations have constructive descriptions
        for arbitrary dimensions, and there are “dimension‑uniform” viewpoints on Clifford groups.
        <strong>Still open:</strong> a small, usable circuit rewrite theory (generators + relations) that
        stays manageable in composite dimensions, with a clean normal form and an implementation that works in practice.
        (<a href={refs.quditCliffordArbitraryD} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">quant-ph/0506065</a>,
        <a href={refs.quditCliffordIdeal} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1307.5087</a>,
        <a href={refs.cliffordReview} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1810.10259</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>Axiomatization of Clifford circuits in even dimensions (power-of-two and beyond)</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">95/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">80/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Pin down what “breaks” in even dimensions (where modular arithmetic behaves differently),
        then build an axiomatization (rules + normal form) that is tailored to
        <strong>even‑d Clifford circuits</strong> (e.g. ququarts <em>d=4</em>, or more generally <em>d=2^k</em>).
      </p>
      <p>
        This matters because many experimental qudit platforms naturally provide access to higher transmon levels
        (often <em>d=4</em> is a very concrete target), and even‑d is where a lot of “easy” algebraic tricks from odd prime
        dimensions stop being plug‑and‑play.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> there are general (dimension‑uniform) Clifford characterizations and reviews, and there
        is active hardware-motivated work that compiles qudit operations for even‑d systems (e.g., ququarts in superconducting circuits).
        <strong>Still open:</strong> a rewriting-oriented axiomatization that cleanly handles even‑d subtleties and supports automated normalization.
        (<a href={refs.cliffordReview} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1810.10259</a>,
        <a href={refs.quditCliffordIdeal} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1307.5087</a>,
        <a href={refs.transmonQuditSynthesis} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2212.04496</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h3>Other structured fragments</h3>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>
      Axiomatization of CNOT-dihedral circuits for qupits/qutrits (Pauli + controlled-addition + phase gates)
    </summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">80/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">75/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Define a qudit analogue of the <strong>CNOT-dihedral</strong> fragment
        (roughly: “linear reversible structure + structured phases”), and then:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>either give a <strong>finite presentation</strong> (generators + relations) with a usable normal form,</li>
        <li>or identify precisely where/why finiteness fails in higher dimension.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters because CNOT-dihedral-style fragments are central to compilation (they capture “phase logic”),
        and a qudit version would directly feed into qutrit/qupit optimizers (especially for diagonal unitaries).
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> for qubits, there is a finite presentation and well-studied synthesis/optimization
        approaches connected to phase polynomials.
        For qutrits/qudits, phase-gadget methods and qudit phase-polynomial optimizers exist, but a clean “qudit CNOT-dihedral”
        rewrite theory is not yet standardized.
        <strong>Still open:</strong> a coherent definition + a rewrite system that is both mathematically solid and practically implementable.
        (<a href={refs.cnotDihedralPresentation} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1701.00140</a>,
        <a href={refs.cnotDihedralSynthesis} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2006.12042</a>,
        <a href={refs.phasePolynomialsQubit} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1303.2042</a>,
        <a href={refs.phasePolynomialsQudit} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1902.05634</a>,
        <a href={refs.qutritPhaseGadgets} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2204.13681</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>Axiomatization of single-qupit Clifford+(T) / cyclotomic circuits (one wire)</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">70/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">75/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Study a single‑qudit analogue of “Clifford+T” (more generally:
        <strong>Clifford‑cyclotomic</strong> gate sets), focusing on one wire:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>choose a concrete gate set (qutrit or general odd prime dimension),</li>
        <li>define a <strong>normal form</strong> and a synthesis algorithm,</li>
        <li>turn it into code (a small exact synthesizer / normalizer),</li>
        <li>and measure “non‑Clifford cost” (the analogue of T-count) on random and structured targets.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters because exact synthesis and cost metrics are crucial for fault-tolerant compilation, and qudit
        versions are much less “standard library” than the qubit case.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> canonical forms and normal forms exist for single‑qutrit Clifford+T and, more generally,
        single‑qudit Clifford+T in odd prime dimensions; there is also active work on qutrit cyclotomic gate sets and
        multi‑qutrit exact synthesis.
        <strong>Still open:</strong> unifying these results into a clean rewrite system and making it “compiler-ready”
        (robust implementation, benchmarks, and integration points).
        (<a href={refs.singleQutritCliffordT} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1803.05047</a>,
        <a href={refs.singleQuditCliffordT} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2011.07970</a>,
        <a href={refs.qutritCyclotomic} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2303.13644</a>,
        <a href={refs.multiQutritExactSynthesis} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2405.08136</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>
      Exploit generalised qudit controlled-X decompositions to improve algorithms (compression/storage tradeoffs)
    </summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">65/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">75/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Use known qudit decompositions (especially controlled‑addition / “CINC”-style gates) to get
        <strong>measurable improvements</strong> in concrete algorithms. A good outcome would be:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>a small “benchmark suite” (e.g., arithmetic blocks, oracles, diagonal gadgets),</li>
        <li>a catalogue of alternative encodings (qubits vs qutrits/qudits),</li>
        <li>quantitative tradeoffs (two‑qudit gate count, depth, ancillas, or runtime/memory).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters because qudits can pack more information per register and sometimes reduce the number of entangling
        operations; but the benefits are often scattered across papers and not systematically translated into compiler passes.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> there is a broad review of qudit advantages and a detailed body of work on qudit gate
        libraries, exact-universal synthesis, and multi-controlled qudit gate constructions.
        <strong>Still open:</strong> a “compilation-minded” evaluation: which algorithmic patterns reliably benefit from qudits,
        under realistic constraints and cost models.
        (<a href={refs.quditsReview} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2008.00959</a>,
        <a href={refs.quditCircuitsUniversal} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">quant-ph/0509161</a>,
        <a href={refs.multiControlledQudit} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2303.12979</a>,
        <a href={refs.transmonQuditSynthesis} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2212.04496</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>"Fault Tolerance by Construction": extend the framework to qudit circuits</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">90/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">95/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Extend the “fault‑equivalence‑preserving rewrite” idea from qubits to <strong>qudits</strong>.
        A concrete deliverable could be:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>a clear definition of fault equivalence for a qudit noise model (or a family of models),</li>
        <li>a small set of <strong>qudit-safe</strong> diagram rewrites (rewrites that preserve fault equivalence),</li>
        <li>a prototype pipeline: specification → diagram → fault‑safe rewriting → extracted circuit,</li>
        <li>case studies on small qudit gadgets or code circuits.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters because “correct-by-construction” compilation is a strong way to avoid accidentally destroying
        the error-detecting/correcting properties of circuits during optimization.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> the Fault Tolerance by Construction framework formalizes fault equivalence and adapts
        ZX-style reasoning to preserve it; separately, qudit fault-tolerant computation is a mature theory area.
        There is also ongoing work generalising graphical calculi beyond qubits (e.g., qudit ZH).
        <strong>Still open:</strong> a clean intersection: qudit-aware fault-equivalence rewrites + extraction + implementation
        and evaluation.
        (<a href={refs.faultToleranceByConstruction} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2506.17181</a>,
        <a href={refs.quditFaultTolerance} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">quant-ph/9802007</a>,
        <a href={refs.quditZH} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2307.10095</a>,
        <a href={refs.zxCompleteFiniteDim} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2405.10896</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>Qubit circuits</h2>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>Clifford+ancilla minimisation (gate count/depth vs number of ancillas)</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">75/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">85/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Build optimisation methods for Clifford(-dominant) circuits that treat
        <strong>ancillas as an explicit resource</strong>.
        Instead of “minimise CNOT count for a fixed number of wires”, the goal is to produce a
        <strong>Pareto tradeoff</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>How many CNOTs / how much depth can you save if you allow 1, 2, … ancillas?</li>
        <li>What changes if ancillas may be reset/discarded (when the model allows it)?</li>
        <li>Can you build a practical heuristic guided by exact methods for small subcircuits (peepholes)?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters because real devices and compilers constantly balance “more qubits” against “fewer noisy two‑qubit gates”.
        Making that tradeoff explicit can lead to better compilation strategies and clearer engineering decisions.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> there are strong exact synthesis and optimisation approaches for Clifford circuits using SAT,
        including tools that target CNOT count/depth; and there are theoretical studies of space‑depth tradeoffs for CNOT circuits.
        <strong>Still open:</strong> a student-friendly, end-to-end framework that (1) exposes the tradeoff as an output, and
        (2) integrates with compiler workflows (benchmarks, slicing, connectivity).
        (<a href={refs.qsynthPaper} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2504.00634</a>,
        <a href={refs.qsynthRepo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Q‑Synth (code)</a>,
        <a href={refs.depthOptimalCliffordSAT} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2305.01674</a>,
        <a href={refs.cnotSpaceDepthTradeoff} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1907.05087</a>,
        <a href={refs.ancillaCostTradeoff} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1405.6073</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>Qudit ZX-calculus</h2>

  <p class="muted">
    The <strong>ZX-calculus</strong> and <strong>ZH-calculus</strong> are diagrammatic languages used to simplify circuits,
    prove equalities, and build automated optimisers (diagram rewriting → circuit extraction).
  </p>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>Circuit extraction from qudit ZX-calculus diagrams (arbitrary d)</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">85/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">90/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Build an extraction pipeline for <strong>qudit ZX diagrams</strong>:
        take a simplified diagram and output an equivalent circuit in a chosen qudit gate set.
        A strong project outcome is:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>a well-defined “extractable” normal form for qudit diagrams (analogue of qubit graph-like forms),</li>
        <li>an extraction algorithm with clear complexity/limits,</li>
        <li>a prototype implementation + benchmarks.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters because rewriting is only practically useful if we can reliably come back to circuits
        that run on hardware (or simulators).
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> for qubits, extraction is supported in mature tooling (notably PyZX) and backed by strong
        simplification techniques; there are also completeness results for ZX and extensions to qutrit stabilizer fragments.
        <strong>Still open:</strong> qudit extraction methods that are dimension-generic and work beyond small hand-crafted cases.
        Also, extraction can be computationally hard in the worst case, so the key is to identify the right “practical fragments”.
        (<a href={refs.pyzx} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1904.04735</a>,
        <a href={refs.zxGraphSimplification} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1902.03178</a>,
        <a href={refs.zxExtractionHardness} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2202.09194</a>,
        <a href={refs.zxCompleteFiniteDim} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2405.10896</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>Circuit extraction from qutrit ZX-calculus diagrams</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">75/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">85/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Build an end-to-end <strong>qutrit</strong> pipeline:
        (1) simplify a qutrit ZX diagram, then (2) extract a reasonably efficient qutrit circuit (in a chosen gate set),
        and (3) benchmark the results (gate count/depth vs baselines).
      </p>
      <p>
        This is important because qutrit ZX theory exists, but practical “push-button” workflows are still much less standard
        than in the qubit world; so this project directly improves the tooling gap.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> the qutrit ZX calculus is complete for stabilizer quantum mechanics, and there are concrete
        constructions for qutrit diagonal gates via phase gadgets.
        <strong>Still open:</strong> robust extraction conventions, good benchmark coverage, and tool integration comparable to PyZX for qubits.
        (<a href={refs.qutritZXStabilizerComplete} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1803.00696</a>,
        <a href={refs.qutritPhaseGadgets} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2204.13681</a>,
        <a href={refs.pyzx} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1904.04735</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="offer">
