      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Develop a diagrammatic rewrite language whose <strong>primitive generators</strong> match
        <em>two-level</em> qudit operations (gates that act non-trivially only on a pair of levels; a common “hardware-native” situation).
        The project should aim to:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>define the generator set and its semantics (as matrices),</li>
        <li>propose a core set of rewrite rules (soundness first; completeness for a useful fragment if feasible),</li>
        <li>show how it plugs into compilation (diagram rewriting → synthesis/extraction in the same gate set).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters because it aligns diagrammatic reasoning with how some qudit hardware is actually controlled, reducing the gap between theory and compilation.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> qudit synthesis methods based on QR/two-level decompositions are well studied, and hardware-motivated compiler work exists for qudits (e.g., transmon qudits).
        <strong>Still open:</strong> a “ZX-like” calculus that uses these two-level primitives directly and supports simplification/verification workflows.
        (<a href={refs.quditCircuitsUniversal} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">quant-ph/0509161</a>,
        <a href={refs.transmonQuditSynthesis} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2212.04496</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
