    <h2>Linear optical circuits (LOv / LOfi)</h2>

  <p class="muted">
    In single-photon encodings, a photon delocalised over <em>d</em> modes behaves like a
    <strong>d-level</strong> system. LOv-style graphical calculi represent linear-optical circuits built from
    <strong>beam splitters</strong> and <strong>phase shifters</strong>, and support rewriting/normal forms.
  </p>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>Generalized Hadamard (Fourier) gates in LOv-calculus (prime & composite dimensions)</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">75/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">75/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Develop a <em>dimension-generic</em> and <em>rewrite-friendly</em> construction of the
        generalized Hadamard gate <strong>H<sub>d</sub></strong> (i.e. the discrete Fourier transform / “mode-mixing” unitary)
        as an LOv/linear-optical circuit (beam splitters + phase shifters), with a clear story for:
        <strong>prime</strong> <em>d</em> (building blocks) and <strong>composite</strong> <em>d</em> (recursive assembly from prime factors).
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Specify the exact convention: which matrix you call <strong>H<sub>d</sub></strong> (phases / global phase),
          and which LOv generators you allow (phase shifters, beam splitters, swaps/identities).
        </li>
        <li>
          Produce explicit circuits for small primes (start with <em>d=2,3,5</em> and push toward <em>d=7</em>),
          and identify reusable substructures.
        </li>
        <li>
          Implement a generator: input <em>d</em> → output a circuit (plus a verifier that checks the produced matrix matches H<sub>d</sub>).
        </li>
        <li>
          Extend to composite dimensions by a recursive scheme based on prime-factor “oracles”
          (e.g. build H<sub>d<sup>k</sup></sub> from H<sub>d</sub>, and H<sub>dd′</sub> from H<sub>d</sub> and H<sub>d′</sub>).
        </li>
        <li>
          Benchmark resource counts (beam splitters, phase shifters, depth) vs baseline “generic unitary” decompositions.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Why it matters:</strong> Fourier/Hadamard mixing is the central primitive behind QFT-like transforms,
        state preparation, and many photonic interferometer constructions. Having a structured, dimension-parametric
        synthesis (especially for composite <em>d</em>) can become a reusable compiler pass and a good testbed for LOv rewriting.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        LOv/LOfi-style calculi provide a principled rewriting setting for linear optics (including completeness/normal-form
        results in stronger photonic models), and have also been used as a bridge to complete equational theories for
        standard quantum circuits. The missing piece here is a <em>specialised</em>, scalable, implementation-ready synthesis story
        for <strong>H<sub>d</sub></strong> that exploits the Fourier structure rather than treating it as an arbitrary unitary.
        (<a href={refs.lofiCalculus} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2402.17693</a>,
        <a href={refs.equationalTheoryComplete} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2206.10577</a>,
        <a href={refs.equationalTheoryMinimal} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2311.07476</a>)
      </p>

      <p class="refs">
        <strong>Starting point:</strong>
        <a href="/notes/generalized-hadamard-gates.pdf">PDF notes (draft)</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>


  <h2>Alternative mathematical viewpoints</h2>

  <details class="offer">
    <summary>Generalise "With a Few Square Roots, Quantum Computing is as Easy as π" to qutrits (cube roots of unity)</summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="scores">
        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">85/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">70/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Investigate whether the “few square roots” representability phenomenon has a
        <strong>qutrit analogue</strong> (think “few cube roots of unity”).
        A precise end goal is to produce either:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>a theorem + constructive algorithm showing which qutrit unitaries are representable in a small algebraic extension, or</li>
        <li>a clear obstruction (“here is why the analogue fails / needs a different ring”).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters because these algebraic characterisations often become exact synthesis algorithms (a direct bridge from math to compilers).
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> the “few square roots” phenomenon is formalised for qubits, and for qutrits there is a strong
        body of exact synthesis work for Clifford-cyclotomic style gate sets (including normal forms).
        <strong>Still open:</strong> a clean “qutrit/cube-root” analogue with a crisp ring/normal form story and constructive consequences.
        (<a href={refs.fewSquareRoots} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2310.14056</a>,
        <a href={refs.qutritCyclotomic} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2303.13644</a>,
        <a href={refs.singleQuditCliffordT} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2011.07970</a>,
        <a href={refs.multiQutritExactSynthesis} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:2405.08136</a>)
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

