        <strong>Scores:</strong> Difficulty <span class="pill">80/100</span> · Importance <span class="pill">65/100</span>
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Goal:</strong> Survey and extend algebraic frameworks that represent qudit states and gates via
        <strong>generalised Clifford algebras</strong> (or geometric-algebra-like formalisms), and connect them to modern tooling:
        stabilizers, circuit identities, ZX/ZH-style reasoning.
      </p>
      <p>
        This matters because a good algebraic representation can turn “hard matrix identities” into “easy algebra,”
        which can then feed into new compilation or verification methods.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>State of the art:</strong>
        <strong>Already done:</strong> there is work developing graphical/algebraic calculi for multi-qudit computation grounded in generalised Clifford algebras,
        and broad references on Clifford groups across dimensions.
        <strong>Still open:</strong> turning these viewpoints into practical compiler representations or rewrite systems that outperform or complement ZX/ZH approaches.
        (<a href={refs.quditCliffordAlgebras} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1809.07809</a>,
        <a href={refs.cliffordReview} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">arXiv:1810.10259</a>)
      </p>
